er":"C","text":"<div><p>Value of <em>K </em>for marginal stability = 8.16</p></div>","isCorrect":true},{"letter":"D","text":"<div><p>For <em>K </em>&gt; 0 system must be stable.</p></div>","isCorrect":false}],"nat":{"low":"","high":""},"solution":"<p><img src=\"/media/b42ebbccabb314d7ceed8f9bd7fbc4b7b2f0cd46.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p>\n<p><img src=\"/media/bb902914aa42b3c13ec23577f24d4f3510171bb3.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p>","subject":"multi | Marks +1"},{"qnum":15,"type":"MCQ","correct":"A","right":2,"wrong":0.66,"qtext":"<p>The open loop transfer function to the given root locus diagram (0 &lt; <em>K </em>&lt; &infin;) is</p>\n<p><img src=\"/media/c6a90360f9d55455631e0f7c1f6d5469623165c2.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p>\n<p> </p>","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"<div><p>&ndash;60&deg;</p></div>","isCorrect":true},{"letter":"B","text":"<div><p>&ndash;30&deg;</p></div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"C","text":"<div><p>120&deg;</p></div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"D","text":"<div><p>60&deg;</p></div>","isCorrect":false}],"nat":{"low":"","high":""},"solution":"<p><img src=\"/media/36693e672903e99309aaa8795fb4419edfbdbb5a.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p>","subject":"single | Marks +2 / -0.66"},{"qnum":16,"type":"MCQ","correct":"D","right":2,"wrong":0.66,"qtext":"<p>The step response of a second order system is<em> c(t)</em> = 1 + 0.5<em>e</em><sup>&ndash;30<em>t</em></sup> &ndash; 1.5<em>e</em><sup>&ndash;10<em>t</em></sup>. What is the damping ratio of the system?</p>","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"<div><p>2.31</p></div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"B","text":"<div><p>0.87</p></div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"C","text":"<div><p>1</p></div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"D","text":"<div><p>1.15</p></div>","isCorrect":true}],"nat":{"low":"","high":""},"solution":"<p><img src=\"/media/6f30625abe4665e680bae7e7cd327e7734737f80.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p>","subject":"single | Marks +2 / -0.66"},{"qnum":17,"type":"NAT","correct":"","right":1,"wrong":0,"qtext":"<p>The root locus diagram of a control system is shown in figure below:</p>\n<p><img src=\"/media/3a1b6f69915c5a95d578edee72b3914a520dca1a.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p>\n<p>The exact coordinate of the breakaway point on the root locus diagram as the gain &lsquo;<em>K</em>&rsquo; varies from 0 to &infin; is _______ .</p></div>\n<div><span>Numerical answer type</span>","options":[],"nat":{"low":"-3","high":"-3"},"solution":"<p><img src=\"/media/30087a41bd28def977961976722f6e23c2e19135.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p>\n<p>Since this point lies between the real open-loop poles at &ndash;2 and &ndash;4, confirming it as a valid breakaway point.</p>","subject":"numerical | Marks +1"},{"qnum":18,"type":"MCQ","correct":"B","right":1,"wrong":0.33,"qtext":"<p>If the roots of a second order characteristic equation are given by <em>s</em><sub>1, 2</sub> = &ndash;3 &plusmn;<em>j</em>2, then the values of damping ratio &xi; and the damped natural frequency &omega;<sub><em>d</em></sub> are respectively</p>","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"<div><p><img src=\"/media/36f55edd9e8038687dbd61e7f87b928cc966f9ce.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p></div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"B","text":"<div><p><img src=\"/media/bef5e93a93ca62960b04b602f5f2eaea2d012d55.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p></div>","isCorrect":true},{"letter":"C","text":"<div><p><img src=\"/media/6268ecb30d610c4486c5149fda40b59698d2bd54.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p></div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"D","text":"<div><p><img src=\"/media/46c43bc8ceef3401c1b5f23f00cb17f1ec8d9a81.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p></div>","isCorrect":false}],"nat":{"low":"","high":""},"solution":"<p><img src=\"/media/b768bd84ba99d732d54aa047353f7718ddb36363.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p>\n<p><img src=\"/media/f2c69b5d6747096917fa52adb2a66eb749266315.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p>","subject":"single | Marks +1 / -0.33"},{"qnum":19,"type":"MCQ","correct":"A","right":1,"wrong":0.33,"qtext":"<p>The open loop pole zero plot of a negative feedback system is shown below:</p>\n<p><img src=\"/media/bd6cc109904e1cfecb5551478bcae1587821d83d.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p>\n<p>The appropriate root locus plot of this system can be</p>\n<p><img src=\"/media/3d915aadca51d08c03d98b4a17b1ab121c7312c5.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p>","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"<div><p>(a)</p></div>","isCorrect":true},{"letter":"B","text":"<div><p>(b)</p></div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"C","text":"<div><p>(c)</p></div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"D","text":"<div><p>(d)</p></div>","isCorrect":false}],"nat":{"low":"","high":""},"solution":"<p>Root locus always starts from open loop poles and terminates at open loop zeros. Also, a point on the real axis lies on the root locus if the total number of poles and zeros to the right of this point is odd. Thus, only option (a) satisfies.</p>","subject":"single | Marks +1 / -0.33"},{"qnum":20,"type":"MCQ","correct":"A","right":1,"wrong":0.33,"qtext":"<p>The Nyquist plot of an open-loop transfer function of a negative feedback control system is shown below.</p>\n<p><img src=\"/media/5f71f0f26dc203dcd631e519621c19ca42629837.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p>\n<p>If the open-loop system is stable, then the closed loop system will be</p>","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"<div><p>unstable with two RHS poles</p></div>","isCorrect":true},{"letter":"B","text":"<div><p>unstable with one RHS pole</p></div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"C","text":"<div><p>unstable with three RHS poles</p></div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"D","text":"<div><p>stable</p></div>","isCorrect":false}],"nat":{"low":"","high":""},"solution":"<p><img src=\"/media/bb4d6ac67b2363e0a467a0207ed16b105d6ab444.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p>","subject":"single | Marks +1 / -0.33"},{"qnum":21,"type":"NAT","correct":"","right":1,"wrong":0,"qtext":"<p>The Bode magnitude plot of a lag compensator is given in the figure below:</p>\n<p><img src=\"/media/3ee7beda9b00a9a47a48962b2882bdb27d1d708c.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p>\n<p>The angular frequency at which the phase lag provided by this compensator will be maximum is ________ rad/sec.</p></div>\n<div><span>Numerical answer type</span>","options":[],"nat":{"low":"3.6","high":"3.9"},"solution":"<p><img src=\"/media/80a60a1c5e5d17f5d785ff83830c37f3cc62ea52.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p>\n<p><img src=\"/media/524f3c600baedf164e83b42d690eaaa30a646510.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p>","subject":"numerical | Marks +1"},{"qnum":22,"type":"MCQ","correct":"B","right":2,"wrong":0.66,"qtext":"<p>Consider the block diagram shown below:</p>\n<p><img src=\"/media/90eef52ce7972da4ae49aa116cc0df06227dbc56.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p>\n<p>The given system is said to be</p>","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"<div><p>a non minimum phase system</p></div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"B","text":"<div><p>an unstable system</p></div>","isCorrect":true},{"letter":"C","text":"<div><p>a stable system</p></div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"D","text":"<div><p>a marginally stable system</p></div>","isCorrect":false}],"nat":{"low":"","high":""},"solution":"<p><img src=\"/media/d306b69f0ca64a163dfaa8a3e38e7a273706ebaf.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p>\n<p><img src=\"/media/0b15afad9a047f2bb2d969fc6555700d290958bd.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p>\n<p><img src=\"/media/346c063d1e6948058e04dcdc01ff095c221bf652.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p>\n<p> </p>","subject":"single | Marks +2 / -0.66"},{"qnum":23,"type":"MCQ","correct":"B","right":2,"wrong":0.66,"qtext":"<p>The open transfer function of a unity negative feedback control system is given by</p>\n<p><img src=\"/media/89351381fa8ce75e991ca6a771e1ce246bc95636.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p>","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"<div><p>GM = 0 dB and PM = 180&deg;</p></div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"B","text":"<div><p>GM = 0 dB and PM = 0&deg;</p></div>","isCorrect":true},{"letter":"C","text":"<div><p>GM = &infin; and PM = 0&deg;</p></div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"D","text":"<div><p>GM = &infin; and PM = 180&deg;</p></div>","isCorrect":false}],"nat":{"low":"","high":""},"solution":"<p><img src=\"/media/a344094ecb17fcd83ab9dd3f28805a0e3796d5f1.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p>\n<p><img src=\"/media/56f88ae20fdb8998ef63a639e84ff7eb56c40776.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p>","subject":"single | Marks +2 / -0.66"},{"qnum":24,"type":"NAT","correct":"","right":1,"wrong":0,"qtext":"<p>The transfer function of a lag compensator is given as,</p>\n<p><img src=\"/media/a713661f6382bc28ca739b854af9fc59e39dbae0.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p>\n<p>If this lag compensator is designed by the RC circuit shown below, then the value of &alpha; will be______.</p>\n<p><img src=\"/media/7a76249e6cb416eae7d7d9008bad998c115f59fa.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p></div>\n<div><span>Numerical answer type</span>","options":[],"nat":{"low":"2.45","high":"2.55"},"solution":"<p><img src=\"/media/db5b8d29dfdc9dc1776b25fcbcda8d22bb37c1e7.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p>\n<p><img src=\"/media/b17d056d964b2ed01a74631a44b03c46055707c5.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p>","subject":"numerical | Marks +1"},{"qnum":25,"type":"MCQ","correct":"A","right":2,"wrong":0.66,"qtext":"<p>Determine the transfer function <em>C</em>(<em>s</em>)/<em>R</em>(<em>s</em>) for the signal flow graph of the system shown below using Mason&rsquo;s gain formula :</p>\n<p><img src=\"/media/2a21f35139254d6dbf7b7bc47d682d2d5b943ed8.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p>","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"<div><p>(a)</p></div>","isCorrect":true},{"letter":"B","text":"<div><p>(b)</p></div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"C","text":"<div><p>(c)</p></div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"D","text":"<div><p>(d)</p></div>","isCorrect":false}],"nat":{"low":"","high":""},"solution":"<p><img src=\"/media/2626d95e3b126c2e505fb275bfb84bf78b6e843f.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p>","subject":"single | Marks +2 / -0.66"},{"qnum":26,"type":"MCQ","correct":"D","right":2,"wrong":0.66,"qtext":"<p>A PID control system is shown below :</p>\n<p><img src=\"/media/fb524650d4787a2ef72417f5ddbb64510391f134.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p>\n<p>For